# mb131, the round-the-block walker (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle mb131
revision 1
state ready
dialect mirebridge
accent light broad
tier standard
written_for rmx_dagfinn_thornham
written_by claude
title the round-the-block walker
fits stage=adult|old
prefers temper=calm humour=none
traits temper=calm talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk walks_once_round_the_block_before_answering_a_slight
likes crisp_apples long_walks sharp_knife warm_rain
dislikes salt_wind haggling
fears a_slight_never_taken_back a_bench_never_offered
hopes a_bench_of_my_own a_letter_delivered
topics pastimes +0.4 weather +0.3 work +0.3 food +0.2 land +0.2 home +0.2 prices -0.3 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=walk gossip=reluctant refuse=plain tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# walks the city end to end with no errand, and goes once round the block before answering any word said crooked: patient, plain and steady, quick to feel a slight and slow to say so, fen-born, with an apple in one pocket and a sharp knife in the other.

words
oath! = Core and cobble | Pips and pavements | By the long way round
saying! = Walk it round before you say it | The long way cools a hot head | A shut door's only shut for today
apple = a hard green apple | an apple with a crack to it when you bite
walk = a long walk | a wander with no errand in it
block = the block | the same old block
corner = the corner | the next corner
knife = my knife | a blade you could shave with
warmrain = a warm rain | rain warm as a bath
saltwind = the salt wind | a wind off the water with salt in it
streets = the streets | every street in the city
street = the street | my street
works = the Works | the yards down the Works
yards = the yards | the yard gates
boots = my boots | these walking boots
crooked = something crooked | a crooked word
haggle = haggling | a stall-keeper's haggle
bench = a bench | a bench to work at
peel = the peel | the peel in one long curl
city = the city | this city

any
opinion @1 houses against 1 : I've asked at every one of #yards#. Empty benches behind every gate, and still no room for a fen-born wolf at any of them.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : A stall adds a copper whenever it reckons your eyes are elsewhere. Mine never are, and I pay the copper anyway. That's what galls.
opinion @1 city against 1 : I've walked #streets#, all of them. Plenty of doors. Not many that open, #pet#.
opinion @1 watch mixed 1 : I've no quarrel with the Watch. We both wear out #boots# on the same cobbles; they're paid for theirs.
opinion @1 strangers mixed 1 : A stranger's no worse than a neighbour till one of them says #crooked#. Then I walk round #block# and see.
opinion @1 road for 1 : I'd walk any road you put in front of me. A road never says #crooked# it has to take back.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. I'm only passing. I'm always only passing. | #greet#, #address#. Walking, are you? So am I.
line @2 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. I've done two streets already. | #greet#. Early, and #boots# are on.
line @3 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late for a walk. Not for me, mind. | #greet#. The lamps make #streets# look shorter.
line @4 greet [here.weather=rain] : #greet#. Wet, and I'm out in it anyway. | #greet#. #rain.capitalize#. I'll walk in it; I don't mind.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : So it's you. I came this way on purpose, #pet#. | You. Good. I came round #corner# hoping.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. We're walking the same streets, #address#. | You again. I've been round #block# since; it's still you.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Walk with me a bit. | #greet#. I was coming round to you next.
line @3 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Mind the dark corners. | #greet#. Late for both of us.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | #farewell#. I'm off round #block#.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Keep under the lamps. | #s8_night#.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | #s8_byeback#.
line @1 react.news : #ack#. | That's a thing to walk on, #address#.
line @1 react.news.new : #surprise#. Never heard it. | That's new. I'll turn it over round #block#.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. | I had it on my walk this morning, two streets down #street#.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. I'll want it said twice. | That sounds like #nonsense# to me till somebody shows it plain.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : That's hard. #pity#. | #sympathy#. I'm sorry for them.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. Good. | That's #good#. Somebody's had a fair turn.
line @1 answer.how : #fine#. I've walked half #city#. | Steady. Nobody's said #crooked# to me today, yet.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Truly, now, #address#. | How's yourself, #address#? Walking much?
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Walk with me; it helps. | That's a hard road, #address#. Come round #block# with me.
line @1 reassure : #reassure#. It'll come right. | It'll ease. Give it #walk# and a night's sleep.
line @1 take_offence : That's #crooked#, that. I'll go round #block# and come back. | I heard that. Give me as far as #corner# before I answer it.
line @1 accept_apology : Round the block and back, and it's gone. #forgive#. | It's walked off. We'll say no more, #address#.
line @1 decline : #hedge#. Not today; I've a walk in my legs and nowhere else to put it. | I'll say no, #address#, and not out of spite. I've a thing to see to across #city#.
line @1 accept_decline : #no_matter#. | Another day, then. I'm out on #streets# most days.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. | That's #good# of you. I'll walk off proud.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect#. | Nothing to it. I was coming past on #walk#.
line @1 preclose : Well. #preclose#. | My legs want walking. I'll be off round #corner#.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. #farewell#. | Off you go. I'll take the long way, #pet#.
line @1 give.advice : #advise#: say nothing hot. Walk it round first. | Go the long way. #saying#.
line @1 ack : #bc_it_is# | #bc_true#
scene the_long_way
when topic=pastimes|day self.likes=long_walks
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : I walked from #works# up to the chapel steps and back this morning. No errand. Just #walk#. | I go out with no place in mind and come back when #boots# say so. | There's not a street in #city# I've not walked twice, #address#.
b : What for? | How far did they say? | Twice?
a : For the walk. It's the one thing I do that nobody has to pay for, #pet#. | Past the chapel, down the long rope sheds, and home by the wharf. #boots.capitalize# have opinions. | Twice, and some of them ten times. You see more of #streets# the second time.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_clever#

scene once_round
when topic=smalltalk|friends
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Somebody said #crooked# to me yesterday. I went round #block# before I answered. | When a word stings, I walk it round. By #corner# I've mostly forgiven it. | You'll see me walk off sometimes, partway through a talk. It's not rudeness. It's the opposite.
b : And when you got back? | Mostly? | The opposite?
a : I said what I'd meant to say, only cooler. Worked, too, #oath#. | Mostly. A few take two goes round. #saying#. | It's me not saying a thing I'd have to take back. I did that once, and I'll not again.

scene warm_rain_out
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring self.likes=warm_rain
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess / ack
a : #warmrain.capitalize#. Hood down and straight out into it. | Now there's a rain worth having. Warm, and it smells of the stones. | After a dry week, #warmrain# is the best walk there is.
b : You'll be soaked. | I can smell it too. | Best walk?
a : I'll dry. That's the beauty of #warmrain#. | It's the dust coming up off the cobbles, #pet#. | #streets.capitalize# empty and I have them to myself. #oath#.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_lovely# | #bc_fair#

scene salt_in_the_fur
when topic=weather|land here.region=ridgemere here.weather=overcast|clear|storm self.dislikes=salt_wind
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : #saltwind.capitalize# again. It sits in my fur a week after. | Wind's off the water today. I'll walk inland, up the hill, out of #saltwind#. | Salt on my lips from walking once past the harbour, #address#.
b : It's the sea. | Up the hill's no better. | Nobody minds it after a while.
a : I know it is. I didn't grow up by one, and it shows, #pet#. | It's better by a street or two. I've measured it in #boots#. | I mind it. I've had my fill of trying to like it.

scene apple_in_the_pocket
when topic=food self.likes=crisp_apples
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : I always walk with #apple# in my coat. One for halfway. | The best apple's the one that cracks loud enough to turn heads in #street#. | I bought a dozen hard green ones. Soft apples are #worthless# to a walker.
b : Why halfway? | Loud enough? | No use?
a : By halfway I've earned it, and the second half's shorter with #apple# in me. | A good one, you hear across #street#. That's how you know. | You can't walk with a soft one. It bruises in the pocket and sulks, #address#.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene peel_in_one
when topic=pastimes self.likes=sharp_knife
does tell.boast / react.self / assess
a : I can take #peel# off an apple with #knife#. Never broken it yet. | Keep a knife sharp and it never slips. The dull ones are the ones that bite you. | I stropped #knife# last night till it'd shave a hair off my wrist.
b : Show me sometime. | That's true enough. | Careful.
a : Bring an apple and I will. You can keep #peel#. | Sharp's safe. Most folk have it backwards, #address#. | I'm always careful. That's why it's sharp, #pet#.

scene no_bench
when topic=work self.job=none !self.workplace
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I've no post just now. I walk #yards# every morning and ask. | Nine yards down #works# yesterday. Nine noes. I counted them, walking home. | I'll take any #bench# that's offered. None is, yet.
b : Nothing at all? | Nine? | Will one turn up?
a : Nothing that'll take a fen-born wolf, #pet#. I'll ask again tomorrow. | Nine. Polite, most of them. That's worse, somehow, #address#. | One will. #saying#. I'll be walking past it when it does.
b : #bc_hope_so# | #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_hope_so#

scene the_empty_benches
when topic=times|work here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / concede / ack
a : Four empty benches in one yard, I counted. And a sign up saying no hands wanted. | The Houses have more work than hands, and still say no at #yards#. | You can see the empty benches through the yard doors. I walk past slowly, on purpose, #oath#.
b : Maybe they've no coin for it. | Why say no? | That's a bit much.
a : Maybe. They've coin for the paint on the doors, mind, #address#. | My voice, I'd guess. They hear #self.from_town# in it and stop listening. | It is a bit much. I'll walk it round #block# before I say anything I'd regret.
b? : #bc_maybe# | #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_fair#

scene no_haggling
when topic=prices|work self.dislikes=haggling
does give.opinion / disagree / concede / ack
a : I don't haggle. I ask the price, and I pay it or I walk on, #address#. | A stall-keeper tried #haggle# on me this morning. I walked round #block# instead. | #haggle.capitalize# is two folk lying to each other till one gets tired.
b : You'd save coin if you did. | Did they come down? | That's how markets work, though.
a : A penny, maybe. I'd lose more than that in temper, #pet#. | When I came back round, the price had come down on its own. #oath#. | Then I'll buy where the price is written up plain, and not argue it.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair#

scene the_causeway
when topic=memories|land self.away=yes self.from=fenshore
does tell.memory / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Back in #self.from_town# I walked the causeway every evening. Out to the end and back, reeds both sides. | My legs learnt walking on the boards in #self.from_town#. #streets.capitalize# here are easy after that. | #self.from_town# is flat as a plate. You can walk all day and see where you started.
b[from=fenshore] #fen : The causeway at dusk. I can see it. | The boards. Slippy in winter. | Flat as a plate, and wet as one.
b : All the way to the end? | Easier how? | Is that good?
a@fen : With the herons stood about like they owned it. They did, #address#. | Slippy, and you learn to walk soft. #bc_it_is# | Wet as one. I walk the hills here to make up for it.
a : To the end, where the water starts. Then back, slower, in the #fog#. | Cobbles don't move under #boots#. The boards did. | Good for thinking. Bad for getting anywhere, #pet#.

scene down_the_works
when topic=home|day here.region=ridgemere self.quarter=works
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Down #works# folk say what they mean, and they say it loud. I like that, mostly. | #street.capitalize# smells of hot iron by noon. You stop noticing it after a year. | Nobody down #yards# asks where you're from. They ask can you lift.
b : Mostly? | A year? | Can you?
a : Mostly. Loud and crooked's still crooked. I walk round #block#, same as anywhere. | A year, near enough. Now I miss it on a Restday, #oath#. | I can. They just don't ask me twice, yet, #address#.

scene my_friend
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.friend# is the one I'd walk the whole of #city# for, end to end. | If I'm offended, #self.friend#'s the one who waits while I walk round #block#. | There's nobody I'd sooner walk beside than #self.friend#. We don't talk. We don't need to.
b : That's a good friend. | That's patience. | That's the best kind.
a : #bc_it_is# Rarely said, and meant. | Patience, and I don't thank them enough for it, #pet#. | The best. Quiet company's the hardest to find in #city#.

scene back_pew
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Restdays I take the long way to the chapel. By the steps I've done my praying. | The Bright One hears me best when I'm walking. I'm sure of that, #address#. | I'm not one for kneeling. I talk to the Bright One round #block#.
b : And when you get there? | Why walking? | Round the block?
a : I sit and listen. The walking's the asking; the sitting's the waiting. #saying#. | Because I'm calm then. A calm prayer's a better one, #pet#. | Once round, and I've said it. Twice, if it's a big thing.

scene night_streets
when topic=day|pastimes here.time=night
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : I walk at night when I can't sleep. #streets.capitalize# are mine then. | You hear #city# breathing after dark. The pumps, the rain, somebody snoring. | A night walk's the best kind. Nobody about to say #crooked#.
b : Is it safe? | Snoring? | Nobody at all?
a : Safe enough, if you keep to the lamps and walk like you know #streets#. | Through an open window, three streets from here. It's a comfort, #address#. | Nobody but the Watch, and they nod. That's all I ask.
b? : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair#

scene fog_walk
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : #fog.capitalize# like this, I walk by counting corners. Seven to the chapel. | Couldn't see my own #boots# this morning. Walked anyway. | Fog's good walking. You can't see the end, so you don't hurry it.
b : Seven? | Did you get lost? | Don't you get lost?
a : Seven, and a step to mind at the fourth. I know #streets# blind. | Once. I fetched up at the harbour instead of the market. Not far off, #oath#. | Never. I've walked #streets# too often to get lost now.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha# | #bc_clever#

scene new_face_walking
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / ask.opinion / give.opinion / ack
a : There's somebody new two doors down #street#. I've walked past three times to get a look. | New folk always go the short way. They'll find out, #pet#. | Somebody new stopped me for directions this morning. I walked them there; I was going anyway.
b : Friendly, are they? | Find out what? | Going anyway?
a : Hard to say from walking past. I'll say #greet# next time. | That the short way's steep, and the long way's flat. #saying#. | I'm always going anyway. I've no place to be, #address#.
b? : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair#

scene word_not_passed
when topic=gossip
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : I hear plenty on #walk#. I don't carry it. | Folk talk in doorways when you walk past. I keep walking, #address#. | #secret.capitalize#. I don't pass on what I've heard over a wall.
b : Not even the good bits? | Why not stop? | Never?
a : The good bits least of all. Somebody always gets hurt by the good bits, #pet#. | Because I'd hear #crooked#, and then I'd have to walk round #block#. | Never, #pet#. A word passed on comes back bent.
b? : #bc_fair# | #bc_ha# | #bc_wise#

story the_unbroken_peel [topic=pastimes|memories self.age>=25]
  : An old basket-weaver taught me to take #peel# off in one go. Said if it broke I'd marry the next wolf I saw.
  : I've never broken one since. Not once, #oath#.
  : I've wondered sometimes if that's why I've stayed on my own.
  ask : Do you hold with any of the old sayings, honestly?
  b : One or two. I'd not admit which.
  b[old] : All of them, at my age. They've had longer to come true.

story the_far_end [topic=memories|land self.away=yes self.from=fenshore self.age>=25]
  : As a #child# I once went the whole length of the causeway in #self.from_town#, till the reeds stopped and the water started.
  : I stood at the end with the #fog# all round and couldn't see either way.
  : I walked back by counting #boots#' steps. Four hundred and eight. I've never forgotten the number.
  b : Four hundred and eight! You'd remember that.
  b[from=fenshore] : That's the old causeway. I'd say about four hundred, myself.

story the_slight [topic=smalltalk|memories self.age>=25]
  : The worst thing I ever said, I said quick, to a friend, and I was wrong.
  : I spent a month saying sorry. After that I started walking round #block# first.
  : We're square now. I still walk round #block#, #pet#.
  ask : Is there a thing you'd give a lot to unsay?
  b : Once or twice. Doesn't everyone?
  b[stage=young] : I say them all the time. I'm working on it.

rule @walk_bark
when concept=bark self.likes=long_walks here.time=dawn|day
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Right. Once round #block#, then we'll see. | Long way, I think. #oath#.

rule @salt_bark
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere self.dislikes=salt_wind
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Fur's full of salt. Inland, then. #annoyed#. | #saltwind.capitalize#. #annoyed#.

rule @warm_rain_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain here.season=summer|spring self.likes=warm_rain
does aside
cooldown 10h
say #warmrain.capitalize#. Good. Hood down. | Smell that on the stones. #good.capitalize#.

rule @apple_bark
when concept=bark self.likes=crisp_apples
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Halfway. #apple.capitalize#. | That's a good crack. #oath#.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. I'm only passing, but go on. | #greet#, #address#. What is it?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. Our streets cross again. What's wanted? | #topic.player#. I've walked past you twice today. Go on, #address#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late, and I'm out walking. #talk_open#
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=none !self.workplace] : No post just now. I walk #yards# and ask. They'll say yes one day. | Walking, mostly. And asking at the gates of #yards#.
dlg @2 resident.job : #job_busy# | This and that, and a lot of walking between, #address#.
dlg @1 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# I'd have heard it on my walk if there was. | Nothing. Half #city# walked this morning, and nothing worth the carrying.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | I walk past doorways. I don't stop at them, #address#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. I'm off round #block#. | #farewell#, #address#. Go the long way.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you walk off in the middle of talking? -> roundblock
node roundblock
say If somebody says a crooked thing, I go once round the block before I answer it. By the corner I've cooled. It's saved me a lot of saying sorry.
mood calm
free
choice Have I said something crooked? -> roundblock_me
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node roundblock_me
say Not yet. If you do, you'll see my back as far as the next corner. Then I'll be round again, and we'll talk.
choice Then I'll mind my words. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
